# topology
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  What happens inside classify_comms() step by step
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Input: node_gpus=[2, 4, 2], pp=2, tp=2, dp=2

  The function answers one question for each comm type:
  "If we run training with this (pp, tp, dp) on this cluster, does each group
   communicate over PCIe (fast) or over the Ethernet switch (slow)?"

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Step 1 — Build the rank→node map
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  node_gpus = [2, 4, 2]  means:
    node 0 (node18, IP 10.10.10.18) has 2 GPUs
    node 1 (node20, IP 10.10.10.20) has 4 GPUs
    node 2 (node16, IP 10.10.10.16) has 2 GPUs

  Ranks are assigned in order: first fill node 0, then node 1, then node 2.

  rank_to_node = [0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2]
                  ↑  ↑  ↑  ↑  ↑  ↑  ↑  ↑
                  r0 r1 r2 r3 r4 r5 r6 r7

  Physical picture:

    node18 (node 0):  rank 0,  rank 1
    node20 (node 1):  rank 2,  rank 3,  rank 4,  rank 5
    node16 (node 2):  rank 6,  rank 7

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Step 2 — Compute global rank from (pp_rank, dp_rank, tp_rank)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  HybridParallelPlugin uses ProcessGroupMesh with axes in order (PP, DP, TP).
  That means the rank formula is:

    global_rank = pp_rank × (dp × tp) + dp_rank × tp + tp_rank

  For pp=2, tp=2, dp=2  →  dp×tp = 4:

  pp_rank  dp_rank  tp_rank  →  global_rank  →  node
     0        0        0           0               0  (node18)
     0        0        1           1               0  (node18)
     0        1        0           2               1  (node20)
     0        1        1           3               1  (node20)
     1        0        0           4               1  (node20)
     1        0        1           5               1  (node20)
     1        1        0           6               2  (node16)
     1        1        1           7               2  (node16)

  Visualised as a 3D cube (PP × DP × TP):

             DP=0                DP=1
          ┌────────┐          ┌────────┐
  PP=0    │ r0  r1 │  TP→     │ r2  r3 │  TP→
          │(n0)(n0)│          │(n1)(n1)│
          └────────┘          └────────┘

             DP=0                DP=1
          ┌────────┐          ┌────────┐
  PP=1    │ r4  r5 │  TP→     │ r6  r7 │  TP→
          │(n1)(n1)│          │(n2)(n2)│
          └────────┘          └────────┘

  n0=node18, n1=node20, n2=node16

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Step 3 — Classify TP groups
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  A TP group = all ranks with the same (pp_rank, dp_rank) but different tp_rank.
  These ranks call AllReduce every single transformer layer → must be intra-node.

  For pp=2, dp=2 there are 4 TP groups:

  pp=0 dp=0:  ranks [0, 1]  →  nodes [0, 0]  →  intra-node ✓
  pp=0 dp=1:  ranks [2, 3]  →  nodes [1, 1]  →  intra-node ✓
  pp=1 dp=0:  ranks [4, 5]  →  nodes [1, 1]  →  intra-node ✓
  pp=1 dp=1:  ranks [6, 7]  →  nodes [2, 2]  →  intra-node ✓

  All 4 groups are intra-node  →  tp_intra_node = True

  Why this is important: TP AllReduce fires once per layer per microbatch.
  If TP goes cross-node that is ~16× slower per layer (100 GbE vs PCIe).
  The pruning rule in search.py rejects any candidate with tp > min(node_gpus)
  precisely because it guarantees tp_intra_node would be False.

  min(node_gpus) = min(2, 4, 2) = 2.  tp=2 ≤ 2  →  safe.
  tp=4 would force some TP groups to span nodes  →  pruned.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Step 4 — Classify PP boundaries
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  A PP boundary = adjacent (pp_rank, pp_rank+1) pair with the same (dp_rank, tp_rank).
  Communication is a P2P send/recv at each microbatch boundary.

  For pp=2 there is one boundary per (dp_rank, tp_rank) combination:

  dp=0 tp=0:  rank 0(n0) → rank 4(n1)  →  CROSS-NODE ✗
  dp=0 tp=1:  rank 1(n0) → rank 5(n1)  →  CROSS-NODE ✗
  dp=1 tp=0:  rank 2(n1) → rank 6(n2)  →  CROSS-NODE ✗
  dp=1 tp=1:  rank 3(n1) → rank 7(n2)  →  CROSS-NODE ✗

  At least one pair is cross-node  →  pp_intra_node = False

  PP cross-node is unavoidable when pp > 1 and there are multiple nodes.
  The cost model uses α_cross + β_cross × activation_size to account for this.
  PP P2P cost is much smaller than TP AllReduce cost because:
    - it only fires once per microbatch boundary, not once per layer
    - it is pipelined with compute in the 1F1B schedule

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Step 5 — Classify DP groups
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  A DP group = all ranks with the same (pp_rank, tp_rank) but different dp_rank.
  These ranks synchronise gradients via AllReduce at the end of each step.

  For pp=2, tp=2 there are 4 DP groups:

  pp=0 tp=0:  ranks [0, 2]  →  nodes [0, 1]  →  CROSS-NODE ✗
  pp=0 tp=1:  ranks [1, 3]  →  nodes [0, 1]  →  CROSS-NODE ✗
  pp=1 tp=0:  ranks [4, 6]  →  nodes [1, 2]  →  CROSS-NODE ✗
  pp=1 tp=1:  ranks [5, 7]  →  nodes [1, 2]  →  CROSS-NODE ✗

  At least one group is cross-node  →  dp_intra_node = False

  DP AllReduce cost is partially overlapped with the backward pass
  (gradient all-reduce starts as soon as a layer's gradients are ready).
  The cost model applies a 30% overlap factor so the effective cost is lower
  than the raw all-reduce time.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Final result for (pp=2, tp=2, dp=2) on cluster [2, 4, 2]
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  ┌───────────┬────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┐
  │ Comm type │         Group example          │           Result             │
  ├───────────┼────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ TP        │ {0,1}, {2,3}, {4,5}, {6,7}     │ ✓ intra-node (PCIe/NVLink)   │
  ├───────────┼────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ PP        │ 0→4, 1→5, 2→6, 3→7            │ ✗ cross-node (100 GbE)       │
  ├───────────┼────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ DP        │ {0,2}, {1,3}, {4,6}, {5,7}     │ ✗ cross-node (100 GbE)       │
  └───────────┴────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┘

  TopologyInfo(tp_intra_node=True, pp_intra_node=False, dp_intra_node=False)

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  How cost_model.py uses this
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Each topology flag selects the right α/β when estimating communication time:

  TP AllReduce:
    if tp_intra_node:  use α_intra, β_intra  (fast)
    else:              use α_cross, β_cross  (slow — candidate gets pruned)

  PP P2P send/recv:
    if pp_intra_node:  use α_intra, β_intra
    else:              use α_cross, β_cross

  DP gradient AllReduce:
    if dp_intra_node:  use α_intra, β_intra
    else:              use α_cross, β_cross

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  What changes with a different plan?
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  (pp=4, tp=1, dp=2) on the same cluster [2, 4, 2]:

  Rank map (dp×tp=2):
    pp=0 dp=0 tp=0 → rank 0 → node0
    pp=0 dp=1 tp=0 → rank 1 → node0
    pp=1 dp=0 tp=0 → rank 2 → node1
    pp=1 dp=1 tp=0 → rank 3 → node1
    pp=2 dp=0 tp=0 → rank 4 → node1
    pp=2 dp=1 tp=0 → rank 5 → node1
    pp=3 dp=0 tp=0 → rank 6 → node2
    pp=3 dp=1 tp=0 → rank 7 → node2

  TP groups:  each group has 1 member (tp=1) → trivially intra-node ✓
  PP pairs:   0→2 (n0→n1) CROSS, 1→3 (n0→n1) CROSS, 2→4 (n1→n1) INTRA,
              3→5 (n1→n1) INTRA, 4→6 (n1→n2) CROSS, 5→7 (n1→n2) CROSS
              → at least one cross → pp_intra_node = False
  DP groups:  {0,1}→nodes[0,0] INTRA, {2,3}→nodes[1,1] INTRA,
              {4,5}→nodes[1,1] INTRA, {6,7}→nodes[2,2] INTRA
              → ALL intra-node → dp_intra_node = True

  TopologyInfo(tp_intra_node=True, pp_intra_node=False, dp_intra_node=True)

  This plan has DP intra-node, meaning gradient sync is fast.
  But more pipeline stages means more bubble overhead (pp-1 bubbles in 1F1B).
  The cost model weighs all these trade-offs and returns the best candidate.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Unit test (no cluster needed)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  from colossalai.auto_parallel.hybrid_planner.topology import classify_comms, describe

  # Test 1: the main cluster plan
  topo = classify_comms([2, 4, 2], pp=2, tp=2, dp=2)
  assert topo.tp_intra_node == True
  assert topo.pp_intra_node == False
  assert topo.dp_intra_node == False

  # Test 2: pp=4, dp=2 — DP becomes intra-node
  topo2 = classify_comms([2, 4, 2], pp=4, tp=1, dp=2)
  assert topo2.tp_intra_node == True
  assert topo2.dp_intra_node == True

  # Print full rank table and comm classification
  print(describe([2, 4, 2], pp=2, tp=2, dp=2))

